WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
How many Edisons does it take to screw in a lightbulb?

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:04.920
How many?

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.000
Who cares? 
He stole the idea and doesn't deserve his own joke.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.920
[generic laugh track]

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.000
Comedy.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.500
Easy, right?
(APPARENTLY FUCKING SO.)

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.540
Comedy is a difficult thing to talk about, because it happens to be so subjective.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.660
There is not one joke that every single person will laugh at and get

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.360
And something that many people might find downright freaking hilarious

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.780
may make you question if you're just a cynical cunt who's lost the ability to find any kind of humor in this world.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:27.280
(Which you definitely fuckin are)

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.100
So I say cautiously that it's very rare that I actually find a comedy anime that I like,

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.340
with only a select handful ever making me audibly laugh out loud.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.220
Looks are a vital part of being a delinquent.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:42.600
Amateurs should try bleaching their hair blonde to look tough.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.580
Be careful. If the chemicals are too strong, your hair will turn white

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:47.920
and you'll look like a pussy.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:48.420
Damn it.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.500
Perhaps this is because my sense of humor doesn't resonate with a lot of the over-the-top

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.560
and almost slapstick-style jokes I see in a lot of gag anime.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.360
My main problem is that most jokes center on one specific punchline or gag.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.720
Now, while they can truly hit, culminating in some of my favorite moments in anime,

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.540
most of the time, they just really miss, or get old really, really fast.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.360
So when Konosuba started to rise in popularity, and I heard about the appeal of its comedy,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.560
it still didn't look like something I would find funny.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.960
And when I eventually sat down and watched it, for the most part... I was right.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.960
It didn't make me laugh that much, and yet, I enjoyed the hell out of it.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.200
I marathoned the entire first season is one sitting.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.120
Konosuba isn't nearly the funniest anime I've seen, for me at least.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:32.420
I never audibly laughed too hard, and I'm not really a big enough fan of the fantasy RPG setting to care about a lot of the parody aspects of it.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:37.140
Yet, despite all this, it managed to remain as one my most consistently entertaining comedies to come out of anime.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:42.100
I may not have laughed hard, but I enjoyed it a lot. Watching the episodes were like consuming popcorn for me,

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.260
and I personally can't think of many casts of characters that I'm as attached to as these ones.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.640
The group are so endearing that I could spend hours just watching their antics on-screen. And I did.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.540
The reason for this is that I actually see some of the same principles I've used when writing my own parodies in the past

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.400
Not that I'm claiming to be a professional comedian or skit writer at all,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.920
but a lot of what makes Konosuba so appealing at its core, is the same thing I see that went into EvAbridged,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:08.200
DBZ Abridged from TeamFourStar™, and a lot of sitcoms you watch,which I don't see very often in anime.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.140
It's something that even YOU can take on and try yourself if you ever want to attempt something like this.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.340
So instead of having some random bozo break down the appeal by researching through the science of comedy,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.260
finding the little intricacies to telling the perfect dick joke,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.900
I thought it would be interesting to analyze it from a perspective from someone who's attempted to write different styles of comedy myself.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.880
You know... the same guy who's given you such masterpieces as

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.800
[melancholy piano]

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:37.220
NERVOUS ERECTION REACHES VAGINA

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:42.460
Now, from an outsider perspective, if may be hard to see the appeal of Konosuba's comedy from just the way the community treats it.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:48.500
You have some kind of parody of the fantasy world setting, some chick who spawns a debate of Schrodinger's Cat with her fucking pants,

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.960
a main guy who seems like a cynical dick,

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:56.260
And a girl everyone seems to be fuckin obsessed with, thanks to something about her and some BIG. JUICY. EXPLOSION.(my waifu ;D)

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:57.720
MAGIC.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.120
So what makes it so popular?

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.480
To its credit, there are a lot of things Konosuba does right, to make it funny.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.380
I haven't mentioned quite a lot before. The actual execution of most jokes are pretty top-notch.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:12.060
The comedic timing is great; it routinely subverts our expectations to parody the fantasy and RPG genre pretty well;

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.680
The animation perfectly accentuates any joke,

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.760
and you can tell how much fun the voice actors are having by the way they deliver the lines being absolutely spot-on.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:26.260
But I'm not here to talk about any of that, as personally, I don't think anything that ruins a joke faster than explaining it, and breaking down its execution.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.200
No. What I'm here to do is break down what makes the comedy in the show tick at its core.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.840
It's a basic thing any of you can take into your own, when writing your own skits, or comedy.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.440
And this all revolves around the cast of characters.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.300
Yes, there core four members of the asshole, the bitch, the weirdo, and the meme

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.300
is what makes this show so endearing, and is the core for pretty much all the comedy in this series.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:51.500
But why is that? Well, to me, what makes the show continually entertaining is just seeing the cast interact with each other as different things arise in the world they are in.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.380
There's no one specific joke, but it's their dynamic and chemistry that carries the comedy.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.340
And by understanding this dynamic, and how to get there, is a technique even YOU can use when you are trying to parody something.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.780
A simple way to approach this is to take a well-known character trait, and completely flipping them on its head.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.180
This is what I personally did with Gendo from EvAbridged,

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.800
turning one of the most sneaky, manipulative assholish characters in a super-serious show, and making him a downright idiot.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.460
Or alternatively, you can take a well-known trait, and crank them up so far that it becomes ridiculous.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.840
This is something quite clearly seen in TeamFourStar™'s Hellsing Abridged,

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.180
as they took the badass immortal Alucard, and cranked up his confidence so much

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.700
that he no longer gives a fuck about anything, or takes anything seriously.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.760
Which works for his character, because why would an OP immortal vampire feel any threat?

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.880
But it also gives opportunity for some entertaining character interactions.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.080
Sup, bitch?
I need to talk to you about some important guests coming today.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:39.840
Are they hookers?

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.340
No.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.880
And like that, you've lost me.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.660
It sounds very simple, but it is highly effective, and you can even see it in play with this lovable cast of idiots.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.720
How do we parody the edgy, powerful paragon of justice protagonist of so many fantasy world shows?

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.380
You flip it on its head, and make Kazuma the cynical everyman with no powers whatsoever.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.280
What about a typical tsundere, valiant knight, and cute mage?

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.600
The tsundere goes so far off the deep end she becomes a self-centered bitch,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.180
the valiant knight becomes so devoted to protecting others it reaches a point of masochism

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:11.040
And the mage is so obsessed with getting Play of the Game she only waits for that one perfect Ult the entire match, and does nothing else.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.640
EXPLOSION!

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:21.200
The important thing is, you need a core cast of characters that work well with each other, and have genuine chemistry,

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.480
with traits that bring to light the elements of whatever traits you are trying to parody in the first place.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:28.560
But you don't do this by thinking of your characters as just a vessel to tell jokes,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.080
but as actual characters.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.320
So for any outsider looking in to the series, it may seem like the explosions and the masochism are just gimmicky memes used for the butt of jokes,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.340
but there is so much more to the cast than that.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:42.300
There is a balance to the four of them. You almost feel like these weirdos were made for each other

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:46.680
and that's the dynamic you should strive for when you're plotting the basic outline to your core characters.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.620
They feel like a group of friends that have this love-hate relationship with each other,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.540
giving off the same kind of banter that you only see with a tight-knit group,

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:55.640
and it's these little interactions that make it so damn entertaining.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.080
So you see? Once you have this baseline set up, the rest is easy,

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:03.720
because you already have the recipe for humorous scenarios by simply having these characters be together.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.100
You don't write jokes. You write characters.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.960
And in doing so, the punchlines and jokes will come naturally out of their basic interactions.(my favorite scene)

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.600
The real trick is to put them in interesting scenarios where their dynamic will be highlighted for the greatest comedic effect.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.180
And while you can introduce more characters to change up this dynamic every so often,

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.340
it will always go back to these core four.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.580
What happens if you don't approach it like this is that you get characters that are basically punchline machines.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:27.260
They exist for no purpose than to throw in a punchline or joke.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:31.260
And while this can work for a time, you've run the risk of it getting old incredibly fast.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.680
Which is why I feel the weakest part of the show by far

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.960
is when they tried to make the punchline Darkness's overly masochistic behavior.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.520
It's predictable, it cheapens her character, and it forces the viewer to watch a girl

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.200
essentially just get off on screen.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.700
I'm sorry, was I talking about negatives? Let me-- let me delete that last line.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:53.360
Aside from this, though, Konosuba is one of the best examples I can think of in anime of a character-driven comedy,

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.040
and it's for this reason that someone like me doesn't even have to find the jokes that funny to be continually entertained.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.940
As watching them always gives you the same feel-good emotions as hanging out with some close friends,

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.440
which is why you get so attached to them.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:06.480
The more we watch, the more inclusive we feel to their relationship.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.520
It's like we are the fifth person in their group.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.640
Laughing along with their antics, rolling our eyes when someone does something stupid,

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.620
and caring in those brief moments when they break out of their molds.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:20.740
It's why they are so lovable as a group, why we get so attached to them, and that's, I feel, is the real strength of the show.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:26.040
Because a lot of people will label this as a simple parody/satire anime, when I think it actually has more in common with a sitcom.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.240
See, the basic structure on sitcoms is following a group of characters as they go about their lives,

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.520
dealing with different situations as they arrive, aka, a SITuation COMedy.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.500
And while there can be progression in character and plot, it is normally at a minimal or a very slow pace,

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.220
drags over many seasons, as the main selling point in each episode

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.580
is watching these characters tackle different situations.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.700
Konosuba has these elements, with the only difference being that the situations they are in

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.640
happen to be revolving around a fantasy world with RPG traits.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.540
It isn't so much that the show is trying to make jokes parodying these elements.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:57.680
It's more that we happen to be watching a very entertaining group of individuals

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.480
as they encounter and tackle these elements.

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:03.840
But by approaching it like this, you actually show that you have a deeper understanding of the things you are trying to parody,

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.760
rather than just merely referencing it.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.500
This is why a show like the Big Bang Theory can be as hated as it is by so many, especially in nerd culture.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.500
It attempts to parody and poke fun at nerds by playing to their grossly inaccurate stereotypes,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:18.260
and thinks that throwing in the odd references shows any kind of understanding of the culture.

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:21.360
I mean, can you imagine if Konosuba tried this approach with a fantasy world setting?

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.380
Alright, then it's settled.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:30.900
We'll save the Sword Art Online world with the Re:Zero inspired No Game No Life Death Tournament on the Log Horizon!

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.040
[another generic laugh track]

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.140
Well, that will be easy. Since Darkness spent all of her stats leveling up her breast size.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:39.280
[more laugh track]

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:40.340
Bazinga!

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:42.460
[even more laugh track]

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:44.320
I'm sorry. Why are you all talking like this?

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.080
I mean... Look at me! I'm Kirito Jesus-kun God-sama!

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:51.620
[laugh track]

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:55.320
Having settled this, keep in mind that this is by no means the only way to write comedy.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.860
What I presented here was just one way to approach it out of many,

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:00.460
and there is no one right way to make something funny.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.080
From my research, this kind of approach is good for a long-running, serialized show,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.900
as we get attached to the characters over time.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:12.160
And while I appreciate well-thought-out character-driven comedies, I can also find very trash degenerate toilet humor hilarious too.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.280
You know. If done right.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.820
There is no exact science to this, but for what it's worth, whether it be for what I talked about,

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:22.440
or for something I didn't cover, I can definitely se why Konosuba is getting the attention that it is.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.360
The characters are lovable, and it gives off this unique charm only a few comedy anime have ever been able to achieve.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:31.360
That even if you didn't find this gut-bustingly funny, there may still be something for you here.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:34.940
And hopefully, you learned something basic about comedy writing in this video, but remember:

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:40.140
If all else fails, you can give up on clever writing and fall back to the one thing you can always rely on.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.420
JoJo memes.

